1
00:00:18,127 --> 00:00:20,788
[SPEAKER_03]: This is Majjhima Nikaya 19.

2
00:00:21,668 --> 00:00:22,748
[SPEAKER_03]: Two types of thinking.

3
00:00:23,988 --> 00:00:28,849
[SPEAKER_03]: And it has a parallel in the Chinese Agamas, which is 102.

4
00:00:28,889 --> 00:00:32,270
[SPEAKER_03]: And I've kind of tried to just join them together.

5
00:00:33,070 --> 00:00:34,270
[SPEAKER_03]: So, and I'll let you know.

6
00:00:36,151 --> 00:00:38,351
[SPEAKER_03]: In the Agama, Agama says something a bit different.

7
00:00:40,432 --> 00:00:41,552
[SPEAKER_03]: It's not much difference, but...

8
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:47,552
[SPEAKER_03]: So, on one occasion, the Blessed One said this.

9
00:00:47,792 --> 00:00:53,276
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, before my enlightenment, while I was still seeking enlightenment, it occurred to me.

10
00:00:53,316 --> 00:00:56,699
[SPEAKER_03]: Suppose that I divide my thinking into two classes.

11
00:00:56,719 --> 00:01:03,323
[SPEAKER_03]: Then, I set on one side, thinking pertaining to sensuality, aversion and cruelty.

12
00:01:03,343 --> 00:01:09,888
[SPEAKER_03]: And, I set on the other side, thinking pertaining to non-sensuality or renunciation, non-ill will and non-cruelty.

13
00:01:15,661 --> 00:01:32,122
[SPEAKER_03]: As I lived, and the Āgama says, in solitude, Pali continues, diligent, persistent, vigilant, a sensual, averse, or cruel thought arose in me, and I understood it like this.

14
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:39,570
[SPEAKER_03]: This class of thinking leads to my own affliction, to the others' affliction, and to the affliction of both.

15
00:01:40,572 --> 00:01:44,800
[SPEAKER_03]: It obstructs wisdom, sides with difficulties, and leads away from nibbāna.

16
00:01:46,465 --> 00:01:50,228
[SPEAKER_03]: When I considered this, that type of thinking subsided in me.

17
00:01:50,949 --> 00:01:57,514
[SPEAKER_03]: Whenever sensual, aversive or cruel thinking arose in me, I abandoned it, tamed it and brought it to an end.

18
00:01:58,655 --> 00:02:00,316
[SPEAKER_03]: And the argument says I did not accept it.

19
00:02:00,777 --> 00:02:01,917
[SPEAKER_03]: I vomited it up.

20
00:02:02,918 --> 00:02:08,223
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I saw that countless evil unwholesome states would certainly arise because of such thoughts.

21
00:02:09,584 --> 00:02:11,245
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I think we'll stop there.

22
00:02:13,951 --> 00:02:17,754
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, we covered this before, dear man, but I guess we can repeat.

23
00:02:19,415 --> 00:02:25,260
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it says that when the thought of sensuality arose in him, what did he do?

24
00:02:28,582 --> 00:02:31,084
[SPEAKER_04]: Did he watch moment-to-moment experience?

25
00:02:31,564 --> 00:02:33,206
[SPEAKER_04]: Did he focus on sensations?

26
00:02:33,306 --> 00:02:34,907
[SPEAKER_04]: Concentrate on nostrils?

27
00:02:35,828 --> 00:02:36,828
[SPEAKER_04]: Did he do any of that?

28
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:38,910
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

29
00:02:40,518 --> 00:02:54,411
[SPEAKER_04]: He recognized them for what they are, and he didn't just stay mindful, but he then established the context of the peril, the danger, the lack of satisfaction, the trap, while those thoughts were present.

30
00:02:55,592 --> 00:03:05,182
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he developed the context, while the thoughts are present, then the context neutralizes them, and they subside, they leave you, they go away.

31
00:03:06,121 --> 00:03:12,383
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't get rid of them, or you don't stop your mind from thinking indirectly, because that's another way of grasping onto the bait.

32
00:03:14,984 --> 00:03:19,325
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the emphasis is on the context, and that's how you will vomit it out, for sure.

33
00:03:22,886 --> 00:03:30,748
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't just, like, okay, oh, this is bad, this is bad,

34
00:03:31,478 --> 00:03:32,499
[SPEAKER_03]: But I think you have to do it.

35
00:03:34,060 --> 00:03:55,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but what I mean is that, like, you could easily think, like, I just... you might not actually understand that it's bad, but you just sort of say to yourself, oh, this is bad, oh, this is problematic, this is harmful, but you're actually just not seeing any problem with it and just kind of trying to do...

36
00:03:56,958 --> 00:03:59,680
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's more about actually trying to understand

37
00:04:00,121 --> 00:04:08,025
[SPEAKER_03]: That's why you have to see that it's... At that end, as the bodhisattva, you know, the Buddha's bodhisattva, he already sees this leads away from nibbāna.

38
00:04:08,546 --> 00:04:12,568
[SPEAKER_03]: He's not... you know, he knows what nibbāna is, he knows what it isn't.

39
00:04:12,688 --> 00:04:13,768
[SPEAKER_03]: This obstructs wisdom.

40
00:04:14,409 --> 00:04:16,850
[SPEAKER_03]: So, he already knows why it is bad.

41
00:04:18,731 --> 00:04:25,815
[SPEAKER_04]: But let's say somebody who is trying to do this practice, who's obviously not a bodhisattva, they can still do it.

42
00:04:26,807 --> 00:04:32,609
[SPEAKER_04]: If they recognize that that first and foremost thing is like, yes, thoughts arise on their own mind, things on its own.

43
00:04:33,009 --> 00:04:37,991
[SPEAKER_04]: Eyes want to see objects, ears want to hear sounds, mind will think thoughts.

44
00:04:38,971 --> 00:04:44,553
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the reason why these thoughts are coming is because you have not understood the context of the danger.

45
00:04:45,113 --> 00:04:49,395
[SPEAKER_04]: And you have not understood the context of the danger because you either lack restraint or

46
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:57,274
[SPEAKER_04]: Either way, you're acting out of the discomfort of the pressure that those thoughts put on you.

47
00:05:08,009 --> 00:05:14,933
[SPEAKER_04]: So, precepts have to come first, sense restraint, guarding of the sense doors, watchfulness of your intentions, as we repeat so many times.

48
00:05:15,633 --> 00:05:23,477
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, when these thoughts arise, you see them, without immediately, impulsively, already being down the rabbit hole of acting out of them.

49
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:31,542
[SPEAKER_04]: And once they become clear on the basis of your restraint, and... and kind of, eh...

50
00:05:32,765 --> 00:05:38,289
[SPEAKER_04]: That aggregate of virtue that has been developed beforehand, you think, okay, there are a problem.

51
00:05:38,409 --> 00:05:45,313
[SPEAKER_04]: These thoughts are a problem in a sense of... A bait is a problem, because I still don't quite know how to not get caught.

52
00:05:45,794 --> 00:05:46,574
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the problem.

53
00:05:47,755 --> 00:05:51,778
[SPEAKER_04]: The fact is, Mara will put the bait in his... in what he controls, because it's his.

54
00:05:53,211 --> 00:05:55,512
[SPEAKER_04]: But for as long as you think, no, no, these thoughts are the problem.

55
00:05:55,552 --> 00:05:56,952
[SPEAKER_04]: I must get rid of these thoughts.

56
00:05:57,613 --> 00:06:01,634
[SPEAKER_04]: You basically are holding the view that Mahārāja has no right to bait you.

57
00:06:01,894 --> 00:06:03,755
[SPEAKER_04]: He has no right to set a trap for you.

58
00:06:04,996 --> 00:06:07,116
[SPEAKER_04]: And that means you're already caught.

59
00:06:07,477 --> 00:06:08,537
[SPEAKER_04]: You're already in that trap.

60
00:06:08,717 --> 00:06:10,478
[SPEAKER_04]: You're already taking what doesn't belong to you.

61
00:06:12,919 --> 00:06:15,940
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the practice of virtue and the samādhi that comes out of it,

62
00:06:16,890 --> 00:06:23,275
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what enables you to have the pressure in thought without acting out, without automatically losing perspective.

63
00:06:23,875 --> 00:06:25,196
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, you can start this practice.

64
00:06:25,256 --> 00:06:28,319
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay, this pressure, is it pleasant or unpleasant?

65
00:06:28,839 --> 00:06:29,540
[SPEAKER_04]: It's unpleasant.

66
00:06:29,600 --> 00:06:30,580
[SPEAKER_04]: Is it for my welfare?

67
00:06:30,961 --> 00:06:35,965
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, would something set up as a trap be for anyone's welfare?

68
00:06:36,625 --> 00:06:39,747
[SPEAKER_04]: If something is genuinely good, would you need to trap people into it?

69
00:06:41,489 --> 00:06:44,271
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you start reflecting like that and these thoughts will subside.

70
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:49,942
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you're not anymore providing that ignorant basis that they require.

71
00:06:51,162 --> 00:06:53,283
[SPEAKER_04]: Same goes for ill-will, same goes for cruelty.

72
00:06:54,604 --> 00:07:00,907
[SPEAKER_02]: So, would you say that you think... so that thinking on that thought,

73
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:06,202
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, yeah, yeah, definitely, definitely, sure.

74
00:07:06,522 --> 00:07:07,442
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's exactly.

75
00:07:07,943 --> 00:07:11,545
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, when it says, oh, you abandon these thoughts, that's how you abandon them.

76
00:07:11,965 --> 00:07:19,630
[SPEAKER_04]: By not choosing them, and by actually still emphasizing, through your mindfulness and right recollectedness, the context of the... No, it's dangerous.

77
00:07:20,510 --> 00:07:22,251
[SPEAKER_04]: Any involvement with the trap is dangerous.

78
00:07:22,532 --> 00:07:30,997
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, it's unpleasant for it to be here and pressure me, but not as unpleasant as being hooked by it, and then dragged for days, and then have to redo everything from the start.

79
00:07:32,091 --> 00:07:36,833
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's unpleasant only because you're still not sure whether you'll be able to restrain yourself.

80
00:07:36,913 --> 00:07:39,955
[SPEAKER_04]: But the only way to become sure is to start restraining yourself.

81
00:07:40,135 --> 00:07:41,255
[SPEAKER_04]: And it is a choice.

82
00:07:41,715 --> 00:07:42,596
[SPEAKER_04]: It's always a choice.

83
00:07:43,116 --> 00:07:53,821
[SPEAKER_04]: Every single time, whenever has anyone in the history of saṃsāra acted out of ill-will or sensuality, it was a choice on the level of their thought.

84
00:07:55,301 --> 00:07:56,462
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but I'll say something.

85
00:07:56,942 --> 00:07:58,103
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, but I'll do something.

86
00:07:58,323 --> 00:08:00,104
[SPEAKER_04]: Every... it cannot be otherwise.

87
00:08:00,244 --> 00:08:00,884
[SPEAKER_04]: That's the whole point.

88
00:08:02,837 --> 00:08:07,967
[SPEAKER_03]: Being able to see that, that I have a choice here, that's already quite a... Well, that's what I'm saying.

89
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:11,533
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you won't start, yeah, you won't start the practice by seeing that choice.

90
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:13,961
[SPEAKER_04]: For that, you need to start seeing your mind.

91
00:08:14,321 --> 00:08:17,544
[SPEAKER_04]: And for that, you need to be established in virtue and get the right view.

92
00:08:18,405 --> 00:08:19,105
[SPEAKER_04]: We said that before.

93
00:08:19,125 --> 00:08:25,410
[SPEAKER_04]: When the suttas talk about the unenlightened bodhisattva, it actually means not completely enlightened bodhisattva.

94
00:08:25,450 --> 00:08:28,192
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't mean enlightened, puthujjana, doesn't know what he's doing.

95
00:08:28,212 --> 00:08:30,234
[SPEAKER_04]: He just roams around the world trying to figure it out.

96
00:08:30,694 --> 00:08:30,774
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

97
00:08:30,794 --> 00:08:34,297
[SPEAKER_04]: It means he already has a pretty good knowledge what enlightenment is.

98
00:08:34,317 --> 00:08:35,978
[SPEAKER_04]: He just hasn't still fully developed it.

99
00:08:36,419 --> 00:08:37,239
[SPEAKER_04]: That's even in here.

100
00:08:37,279 --> 00:08:40,702
[SPEAKER_04]: It's indicated because he already knows what would lead to nibbāna and what wouldn't

101
00:08:42,023 --> 00:08:43,344
[SPEAKER_04]: means you already has the right view.

102
00:08:44,345 --> 00:08:51,972
[SPEAKER_04]: So, any practices that unenlightened bodhisattva does, basically means, well, at least you have to have a right view for even those practices to apply to you.

103
00:08:53,133 --> 00:08:55,595
[SPEAKER_04]: And for that right view, you need to be established in virtue.

104
00:08:57,017 --> 00:09:00,040
[SPEAKER_04]: And, eh... again, and that's on the level of choice, every single time.

105
00:09:01,221 --> 00:09:02,502
[SPEAKER_04]: Right choice or the wrong choice.

106
00:09:03,628 --> 00:09:16,191
[SPEAKER_00]: But even though it is still kind of interesting in that, in the sutta, even he starts... he doesn't start by just directly saying, oh, this thought arose, I just abandon it.

107
00:09:16,471 --> 00:09:16,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

108
00:09:17,051 --> 00:09:24,393
[SPEAKER_00]: He starts by reflecting on the nature of that thought, and then through saying, oh, okay, this is dangerous, this is perilous.

109
00:09:25,167 --> 00:09:32,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know, whenever the thought arises, because he sees... he doesn't need to keep on, like, reflecting about it, he's able to abandon it.

110
00:09:32,253 --> 00:09:39,900
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, the reason why people would immediately jump onto a thought, either to accept it or deny it or cancel it, is because they don't see the mind.

111
00:09:40,341 --> 00:09:43,323
[SPEAKER_04]: So, they are fully identified with that which mind is.

112
00:09:44,104 --> 00:09:47,107
[SPEAKER_04]: So, this reaction is just the result of their identification.

113
00:09:47,747 --> 00:09:52,595
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in order to start actually seeing the difference there, you need to stop acting out, as we say so many times.

114
00:09:52,635 --> 00:09:53,397
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to endure it.

115
00:09:53,517 --> 00:09:59,086
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to endure that pressure, and not act out, not break precepts, and not try to get rid of it, because it's unpleasant.

116
00:10:00,609 --> 00:10:01,230
[SPEAKER_04]: There's no other way.

117
00:10:11,103 --> 00:10:16,767
[SPEAKER_03]: And the simile that was included in that is the simile of the cowherd curbing the cows, his cows.

118
00:10:17,528 --> 00:10:30,156
[SPEAKER_03]: So, just as in the last month of the rainy season, in the autumn, when the crops thicken, a cowherd would guard his cows by constantly tapping and poking them on this side and that, with a stick to check and curb them.

119
00:10:30,917 --> 00:10:31,337
[SPEAKER_03]: Why is that?

120
00:10:31,357 --> 00:10:37,662
[SPEAKER_03]: Because he sees that he could be beaten, imprisoned, fined or blamed if he let them stray into others' crops.

121
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:49,066
[SPEAKER_03]: So, too, I saw in unwholesome states danger, degradation and defilement, and in wholesome states the blessing and purity of non-sexuality or renunciation.

122
00:10:51,388 --> 00:10:53,869
[SPEAKER_04]: What do the cows stand for in this simile?

123
00:10:55,891 --> 00:10:56,291
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind?

124
00:10:59,597 --> 00:11:07,984
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you don't let the mind wander into the presented pastures of sensuality or ill-will or cruelty.

125
00:11:08,004 --> 00:11:09,225
[SPEAKER_04]: You keep an eye on your mind.

126
00:11:10,927 --> 00:11:20,215
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I shall not abandon the context of danger, the peril, the trap, because if I do, it means cows are already grazing in someone else's field.

127
00:11:20,895 --> 00:11:22,777
[SPEAKER_04]: They're already taking what doesn't belong to me.

128
00:11:24,825 --> 00:11:35,591
[SPEAKER_04]: I can say there's nothing real in reality and these cows don't exist, but, well, that won't stop people from beating me if my cows eat in their fields.

129
00:11:35,631 --> 00:11:37,172
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it doesn't matter what my views are.

130
00:11:37,392 --> 00:11:38,113
[SPEAKER_04]: Practically,

131
00:11:39,158 --> 00:11:44,025
[SPEAKER_04]: Letting my cows graze in what doesn't belong to me will lead to mine.

132
00:11:44,385 --> 00:11:46,869
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I'm gonna pay the price for it.

133
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:51,435
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it would be very unwise to ignore that situation.

134
00:11:51,595 --> 00:11:54,619
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he keeps an eye on his cows,

135
00:11:55,440 --> 00:12:16,419
[SPEAKER_04]: So, he practices the sense restraint, the context, clarifies it, protects it, so that the cows don't stray into the fields on account of him lapsing and forgetting what's important and getting distracted by, I don't know, sight, sound, smell, taste, touches around the field where he is.

136
00:12:16,439 --> 00:12:21,444
[SPEAKER_03]: And so, the curbing and the cowherd, the curbing is that thinking, that choice.

137
00:12:21,977 --> 00:12:22,397
[SPEAKER_04]: The choice.

138
00:12:22,477 --> 00:12:23,397
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it's the choice.

139
00:12:23,437 --> 00:12:29,579
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, I can choose to ignore this, which is right in front of me, or I can choose to know.

140
00:12:29,879 --> 00:12:36,261
[SPEAKER_04]: I will think about the context so that the cows stay in on this side of the fence and they don't go over there.

141
00:12:36,901 --> 00:12:37,721
[SPEAKER_04]: How long do I have to do it?

142
00:12:37,781 --> 00:12:39,202
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it doesn't matter.

143
00:12:39,222 --> 00:12:48,504
[SPEAKER_04]: If I have to do it for the rest of my life, I'll do it because, again, if they go into other people's fields, that will be my punishment.

144
00:12:49,453 --> 00:12:53,679
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, as the simile continues later on, you know, other crops will die out.

145
00:12:53,740 --> 00:12:55,923
[SPEAKER_04]: You won't always be there to the same extent.

146
00:12:56,684 --> 00:13:02,373
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there will be time where you can relax, but not where... where the temptations and pressures are right there.

147
00:13:03,972 --> 00:13:09,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, even if there isn't an unwholesome state arisen, like ill-will or craving, you're still thinking about the possibility.

148
00:13:09,914 --> 00:13:12,795
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still reflecting on, yeah...

149
00:13:12,975 --> 00:13:13,596
[SPEAKER_01]: Which possibility?

150
00:13:13,796 --> 00:13:16,517
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility of ill-will or craving arising.

151
00:13:16,577 --> 00:13:17,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

152
00:13:17,197 --> 00:13:18,317
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that's the possibility.

153
00:13:18,337 --> 00:13:28,221
[SPEAKER_04]: I mean, it's like, yes, okay, they're not eating in other people's crops now, but the crops, the sensuality, the people that annoy me, are still around me.

154
00:13:29,261 --> 00:13:31,022
[SPEAKER_04]: And if I lose the context of that,

155
00:13:32,875 --> 00:13:36,117
[SPEAKER_04]: My mind will jump and leap at the opportunity.

156
00:13:37,177 --> 00:13:44,421
[SPEAKER_04]: So, I must emphasize the protecting of my mind, not figuring out or fixing the world or the crops around me that tempt me.

157
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:46,022
[SPEAKER_04]: That's an endless task.

158
00:13:46,942 --> 00:13:51,104
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, there's a thought of renunciation, but that thought is

159
00:13:52,518 --> 00:13:57,322
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not something that you sort of, like, start fabricating... No, exactly.

160
00:13:57,523 --> 00:13:58,203
[SPEAKER_02]: ...manually.

161
00:13:58,343 --> 00:14:00,325
[SPEAKER_02]: It's within the same context, right?

162
00:14:00,345 --> 00:14:09,874
[SPEAKER_04]: That sensuality or... Well, when the sensuality is pressuring your ill-will, choosing to not go down that, and accept that bait, that's the thought of renunciation.

163
00:14:09,894 --> 00:14:10,034
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

164
00:14:10,294 --> 00:14:12,096
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a third thing you do upon that.

165
00:14:12,336 --> 00:14:12,757
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

166
00:14:16,319 --> 00:14:27,206
[SPEAKER_03]: Understanding that sensuality, that direction, is developing that non-sensuality, that dispassion for sensuality.

167
00:14:27,286 --> 00:14:34,551
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, there was a note in the suttas, in the back, in the translation, and it said

168
00:14:36,580 --> 00:14:46,285
[SPEAKER_02]: You can ignore all the notes at the back of my journals.

169
00:14:46,345 --> 00:14:47,506
[SPEAKER_04]: It's all from the commentaries.

170
00:14:47,526 --> 00:14:51,188
[SPEAKER_04]: It has absolutely no connection with the actual dhamma, except on paper.

171
00:14:51,708 --> 00:14:51,968
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

172
00:14:52,529 --> 00:14:53,689
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's always that.

173
00:14:53,749 --> 00:14:57,851
[SPEAKER_04]: And you could see where all these contemporary worldly views stem from.

174
00:14:58,372 --> 00:14:59,032
[SPEAKER_04]: It's exactly that.

175
00:14:59,092 --> 00:14:59,512
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, no, no.

176
00:14:59,532 --> 00:15:00,933
[SPEAKER_04]: See, it's too subtle.

