00:15:05,999 --> 00:15:10,472
[SPEAKER_04]: who would who would have not lived in seclusion, sense-restrained, guarding the sense-doors.

179
00:15:11,442 --> 00:15:14,864
[SPEAKER_04]: Watchful of their intentions, being wakeful, will not see their mind.

180
00:15:15,304 --> 00:15:28,691
[SPEAKER_04]: If they don't see their mind, they will not recognize the whole domain of the middle ground between sensuality, actively thinking sensual thoughts, and not choosing that while those thoughts are present.

181
00:15:28,751 --> 00:15:30,471
[SPEAKER_04]: They will not see that choice.

182
00:15:30,631 --> 00:15:31,572
[SPEAKER_04]: It will not become apparent.

183
00:15:31,592 --> 00:15:37,475
[SPEAKER_04]: So then, the only way they could make sense out of this instruction would be, ah, okay, so I must think the opposite.

184
00:15:38,535 --> 00:15:44,079
[SPEAKER_04]: And they wouldn't understand that that's going too far the other way now, because you will think the opposite with your centrality.

185
00:15:44,739 --> 00:16:03,050
[SPEAKER_04]: It's because centrality is here as your context, you're thinking the loving-kindness thoughts, or asubha, asubha, any disgust to get rid of it, because you're already gripped by centrality, because you already don't see the danger, the peril, because the whole domain of developing the context, it just doesn't exist in the person.

186
00:16:05,729 --> 00:16:09,111
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, and it's the actual opposite of sensual thinking.

187
00:16:09,172 --> 00:16:11,633
[SPEAKER_03]: It's non-sensual thinking, yeah.

188
00:16:11,653 --> 00:16:13,895
[SPEAKER_03]: Something else that you put on top of sensual thoughts.

189
00:16:13,955 --> 00:16:14,776
[SPEAKER_04]: Opposite of hate.

190
00:16:14,876 --> 00:16:15,636
[SPEAKER_04]: It's non-hate.

191
00:16:15,836 --> 00:16:16,817
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not love.

192
00:16:17,678 --> 00:16:18,798
[SPEAKER_00]: And the word is actually...

193
00:16:18,838 --> 00:16:22,801
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember when I first saw that in Pali, I was like, oh, that makes so much more sense.

194
00:16:22,821 --> 00:16:24,382
[SPEAKER_00]: It's kama and nikama.

195
00:16:24,442 --> 00:16:28,225
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, which is ahimsa, ahimsa, ahimsa.

196
00:16:28,365 --> 00:16:30,667
[SPEAKER_03]: Anyway, how can you practice loving-kindness when you're angry?

197
00:16:32,577 --> 00:16:38,562
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, according to the commentaries, that's the best time to practice dharma.

198
00:16:44,407 --> 00:16:53,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is kind of quite important to... like, actually means it's not that obvious.

199
00:16:54,765 --> 00:17:00,850
[SPEAKER_00]: What constitutes a thought of sensuality, basically, or intention of sensuality.

200
00:17:00,890 --> 00:17:06,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already something like you can't just kind of start this practice taking for granted your... Why wouldn't it be obvious to somebody?

201
00:17:07,475 --> 00:17:09,717
[SPEAKER_04]: Why would that still be unclear to a person?

202
00:17:10,618 --> 00:17:15,502
[SPEAKER_00]: If they are... well, they're habitually acting out of... Yeah, they're not restraining the body.

203
00:17:15,522 --> 00:17:15,722
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

204
00:17:15,822 --> 00:17:23,369
[SPEAKER_04]: So, when you start restraining, then it obviously becomes more... Then, you can restrain your speech and then it will become obvious what these thoughts are.

205
00:17:25,275 --> 00:17:25,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

206
00:17:26,256 --> 00:17:35,785
[SPEAKER_00]: But even then, like, yeah, because... and restraint, then, also needs to be, then, understood as restraining intentions of... Yeah.

207
00:17:35,905 --> 00:17:36,986
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

208
00:17:37,427 --> 00:17:38,968
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's... yeah.

209
00:17:39,028 --> 00:17:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just doing random physical practices and observances.

210
00:17:42,431 --> 00:17:42,672
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

211
00:17:52,591 --> 00:17:54,314
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, so the sutta continues.

212
00:17:55,175 --> 00:18:03,868
[SPEAKER_03]: Because whatever Bhikkhu frequently thinks, vittakas, and ponders upon, vicharas, that would become the inclination of his mind.

213
00:18:05,882 --> 00:18:17,247
[SPEAKER_03]: If he frequently thinks and ponders upon sensuality, he has abandoned the thought of renunciation, or non-sensuality, to cultivate the thought of sensual desire, and then his mind inclines to sensual thinking.

214
00:18:18,627 --> 00:18:25,530
[SPEAKER_03]: Likewise, with aversion and cruelty, he has abandoned non-aversion and he has abandoned non-cruelty.

215
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:43,567
[SPEAKER_03]: And the Āgama, Chinese Āgama, adds, a monk who has not abandoned thoughts of sensuality, of ill-will and cruelty, will be unable to free himself from birth, old age, disease and suffering, from tears and weeping.

216
00:18:44,948 --> 00:18:47,189
[SPEAKER_03]: He will not be able to free himself from dukkha.

217
00:18:50,650 --> 00:18:54,152
[SPEAKER_00]: I remember in the Āgama it also said, which I kind of liked the combinations, that

218
00:18:54,591 --> 00:19:00,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever a monk frequently, or whatever person frequently thinks and ponders upon, his mind will delight in that.

219
00:19:01,357 --> 00:19:05,681
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, in the Pali, it says that that will become the inclination of his mind.

220
00:19:05,702 --> 00:19:11,867
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you frequently think and ponder, and your mind delights in that direction, and that inclines your mind.

221
00:19:12,408 --> 00:19:15,751
[SPEAKER_02]: An interesting point that I actually have a question about, because...

222
00:19:17,227 --> 00:19:21,870
[SPEAKER_02]: Especially now, you're quite secluded.

223
00:19:22,791 --> 00:19:30,075
[SPEAKER_02]: So, it's very, you know, you are more confronted with your thoughts, which is, you know, for this practice, pretty good.

224
00:19:30,135 --> 00:19:34,458
[SPEAKER_02]: But then, you can really start to

225
00:19:48,063 --> 00:19:52,086
[SPEAKER_02]: And you, you know, it can be agreeable, at least.

226
00:19:52,486 --> 00:19:52,767
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

227
00:19:53,467 --> 00:19:55,088
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the choice we're talking about.

228
00:19:55,829 --> 00:20:01,994
[SPEAKER_04]: But then, yeah, it's sort of... You can choose to not accept the agreeability.

229
00:20:02,514 --> 00:20:02,734
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

230
00:20:03,615 --> 00:20:06,037
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, yeah, you find your mind thinking those thoughts.

231
00:20:06,277 --> 00:20:09,920
[SPEAKER_04]: So, as I said, you can't just stop it, because in a way, you are not doing it.

232
00:20:10,080 --> 00:20:11,081
[SPEAKER_04]: You're subjected to it.

233
00:20:11,898 --> 00:20:18,141
[SPEAKER_04]: But you are then goading on the mind, carelessly, or showing it the danger.

234
00:20:21,582 --> 00:20:29,425
[SPEAKER_02]: But for that, yeah, you need to really develop further on understanding wholesome and unwholesome, of course.

235
00:20:30,146 --> 00:20:34,067
[SPEAKER_04]: No, you don't need to develop the understanding of it, in the sense of you need to figure it out.

236
00:20:34,968 --> 00:20:39,990
[SPEAKER_04]: You need to develop virtue in the body and the speech.

237
00:20:41,244 --> 00:20:46,626
[SPEAKER_04]: So, not fuel it to unwholesome intentions that you act out upon the level of the body and speech.

238
00:20:47,706 --> 00:20:57,129
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you know, you're withdrawn from the coarse things in the world, but there are still things that, by body and by speech, are rooted in those same intentions.

239
00:20:58,009 --> 00:21:01,130
[SPEAKER_04]: And every single time, on a day-to-day basis,

240
00:21:01,987 --> 00:21:03,148
[SPEAKER_04]: You choose to engage with them.

241
00:21:03,208 --> 00:21:05,211
[SPEAKER_04]: Or not every single time, but most of the time.

242
00:21:05,411 --> 00:21:13,119
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why the the clarity between truly what's wholesome and unwholesome is still not seen, because you are still polluting it with your actions.

243
00:21:13,159 --> 00:21:20,227
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's like it will always be the, eh... you get well restrained by the precepts, it'll be secluded and so on, but then speech as well.

244
00:21:21,847 --> 00:21:27,671
[SPEAKER_04]: Don't just... don't... don't disappear, like mentally, in like a chit-chat or pointless debates and stuff like that.

245
00:21:27,711 --> 00:21:29,853
[SPEAKER_04]: It's great with passion, intention and ideas.

246
00:21:30,333 --> 00:21:31,594
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, yeah, but I'm losing...

247
00:21:31,614 --> 00:21:33,675
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm losing the self-awareness on account of doing this.

248
00:21:34,576 --> 00:21:36,437
[SPEAKER_04]: And it's kind of also impulsive.

249
00:21:37,598 --> 00:21:43,062
[SPEAKER_04]: You know, there's that... if a... if a somebody... if a mind decides to say something, it's like a... where's that sutta?

250
00:21:43,722 --> 00:21:46,144
[SPEAKER_04]: It will be said, because it's like you have to kind of go against it.

251
00:21:46,264 --> 00:21:48,245
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes, some... a very difficult thing to...

252
00:21:51,197 --> 00:22:02,141
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you recognize, okay, so again, you can't necessarily stop that impulse overnight, but you could certainly start, like, you know, directing it away from just careless, pointless, meaningless.

253
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:12,644
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why often even like those eight practice precepts would sometimes be changed and will include those, like, ways of right speech.

254
00:22:14,377 --> 00:22:24,426
[SPEAKER_04]: Like the gossip, the chitchat, the slander, and you abstain from all of that as a practice, not as some sort of religious observance.

255
00:22:25,046 --> 00:22:26,467
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's how you would clarify that.

256
00:22:26,968 --> 00:22:33,073
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, even if you don't make the effort to figure out wholesome as wholesome and unwholesome, you will, because you stop polluting it.

257
00:22:35,268 --> 00:22:44,952
[SPEAKER_03]: So, as I lived in solitude, diligent, persistent, and vigilant, thinking pertaining to non-sensuality, non-aversion, or non-cruelty arose in me.

258
00:22:45,753 --> 00:22:47,373
[SPEAKER_03]: I understood thus.

259
00:22:47,413 --> 00:22:53,176
[SPEAKER_03]: This thinking pertaining to non-sensuality, non-aversion, non-cruelty has arisen in me.

260
00:22:53,356 --> 00:22:58,278
[SPEAKER_03]: This does not lead to my own affliction, or to the affliction of others, or to the affliction of both.

261
00:22:58,558 --> 00:23:02,600
[SPEAKER_03]: It aids wisdom, does not cause difficulty, and leads to nibbāna.

262
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:11,605
[SPEAKER_03]: If I think and contemplate these thoughts, even for a night, even for a day, even for a night and day, I see nothing to fear from them.

263
00:23:13,586 --> 00:23:17,668
[SPEAKER_03]: And the āgama adds, I rapidly developed it, made much of it.

264
00:23:18,048 --> 00:23:24,912
[SPEAKER_03]: If I thought... if a thought was... with non-sensuality arose in me, I intentionally kept on thinking it.

265
00:23:26,012 --> 00:23:27,013
[SPEAKER_03]: And so I just kept on

266
00:23:28,580 --> 00:23:29,541
[SPEAKER_03]: And the āgama says the same.

267
00:23:29,561 --> 00:23:35,946
[SPEAKER_03]: If I had... if I further had this thought, if I intentionally kept on thinking, my body will lose strength and my mind will be troubled.

268
00:23:51,665 --> 00:24:02,474
[SPEAKER_03]: Let me rather keep my mind in check within, continuously dwelling in a calm, unified, elevated, having attained composure so that my mind will not be troubled.

269
00:24:02,494 --> 00:24:02,774
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

270
00:24:02,894 --> 00:24:04,596
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there's an important point there.

271
00:24:06,317 --> 00:24:14,644
[SPEAKER_04]: The difference between the certain implication in Pali and the certain implication in the Agama that describes it.

272
00:24:15,244 --> 00:24:18,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the main thing is that the Agama says that he intentionally

273
00:24:19,671 --> 00:24:23,057
[SPEAKER_00]: ...developed and cultivated these thoughts and kept on...

274
00:24:24,430 --> 00:24:33,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Before he came to the point of, sort of, realizing, okay, if I keep on thinking this forever, now...

275
00:24:33,434 --> 00:24:46,959
[SPEAKER_04]: So, on the basis of that Chinese part, you can see that that fatigue and tiredness comes on account of intentional work of thinking the right context, not simply on account of having thoughts.

276
00:24:46,979 --> 00:24:53,802
[SPEAKER_04]: Oh, I had these wholesome thoughts coming, but these thoughts, basically presence of thoughts, means means fatigue, so I must stop thinking.

277
00:24:54,582 --> 00:24:56,964
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's the tiring part, the work.

278
00:24:57,024 --> 00:24:58,866
[SPEAKER_04]: Not the thoughts in themselves.

279
00:25:14,187 --> 00:25:18,189
[SPEAKER_04]: And again, you can only say, I can rest once you did the work.

280
00:25:18,489 --> 00:25:23,611
[SPEAKER_04]: Not like, oh, well, that work is tiring, thus I shall unify my mind here and not even start with the work.

281
00:25:24,472 --> 00:25:30,375
[SPEAKER_01]: What would be the criteria for discerning, eh... the mind is...

282
00:25:31,516 --> 00:25:34,259
[SPEAKER_01]: It's agitated on account of...

283
00:25:34,539 --> 00:25:35,540
[SPEAKER_03]: Fatigue.

284
00:25:35,720 --> 00:25:37,282
[SPEAKER_03]: Loss of composure.

285
00:25:37,422 --> 00:25:43,709
[SPEAKER_04]: No, not like agitated, not like... Well, you wouldn't be going back to centrality, but it would just not be calm.

286
00:25:44,250 --> 00:25:50,817
[SPEAKER_04]: The mind would be revolving around aspects of dhamma and so on in a wholesome way, but you realize, okay, it's too much.

287
00:25:51,457 --> 00:25:51,878
[SPEAKER_04]: What for?

288
00:25:51,978 --> 00:25:52,719
[SPEAKER_04]: Why am I doing this?

289
00:25:53,145 --> 00:26:02,832
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm doing it to be... well, I started doing it because I withdrew myself from sensuality and ill-will, but I am not going back to sensuality and ill-will, so I might as well stop doing this for now.

290
00:26:04,073 --> 00:26:10,117
[SPEAKER_04]: Not stop thinking, but stop intentionally, kind of, fueling and revolving around those wholesome thoughts.

291
00:26:10,598 --> 00:26:13,119
[SPEAKER_04]: Stop the intentional part of vittāka-vicāra.

292
00:26:14,680 --> 00:26:16,602
[SPEAKER_04]: Not get rid of the vittāka-vicāra altogether.

293
00:26:19,143 --> 00:26:20,103
[SPEAKER_04]: Because that's the purpose.

294
00:26:20,723 --> 00:26:24,504
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not, in and of itself, develop constant asubha and constant disgust.

295
00:26:25,504 --> 00:26:25,664
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

296
00:26:25,704 --> 00:26:29,405
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like some minds are more frequently inclined towards sensuality than others.

297
00:26:30,125 --> 00:26:32,545
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you keep losing the context.

298
00:26:32,565 --> 00:26:32,765
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

299
00:26:32,785 --> 00:26:38,747
[SPEAKER_04]: So, make some effort when sensuality is not present, when you're not under the pressure of enduring it.

300
00:26:38,787 --> 00:26:39,067
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

301
00:26:39,107 --> 00:26:46,408
[SPEAKER_04]: Cultivate the ugly, the repulsive and all of that, so that then context will be there waiting when sensuality comes.

302
00:26:48,245 --> 00:27:01,337
[SPEAKER_04]: Non-sensuality, non-ill-will, non-cruelty, those thoughts, aspects of dhamma, clarity of the presence of the phenomena and so on, thinking that intentionally, fortifies the context.

303
00:27:02,398 --> 00:27:05,201
[SPEAKER_04]: But, you realize, okay, but I am constantly thinking.

304
00:27:07,423 --> 00:27:08,584
[SPEAKER_04]: So, why don't I calm down?

305
00:27:08,944 --> 00:27:13,088
[SPEAKER_04]: Why don't I stop the intentional picking up of these thoughts?

306
00:27:16,926 --> 00:27:18,407
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's why it's actually first jhāna.

307
00:27:19,447 --> 00:27:21,729
[SPEAKER_04]: This, what Agama describes, is the first jhāna.

308
00:27:22,589 --> 00:27:35,456
[SPEAKER_04]: When thinking and pondering has been thoroughly withdrawn from wandering back into five hindrances, sensuality, ill-will, cruelty, and then you recognize that it's quite secluded now.

309
00:27:36,137 --> 00:27:41,520
[SPEAKER_04]: There are no more concerns, joys, worries in regard to the world.

310
00:27:41,660 --> 00:27:45,162
[SPEAKER_04]: All you have is thinking and pondering of a very clear nature that you have

311
00:27:45,683 --> 00:27:53,491
[SPEAKER_04]: Actively developed, in regard to unwholesome states, we drew it from, purified from, and you realize, oh, so I can stop doing this now.

312
00:27:54,713 --> 00:28:01,279
[SPEAKER_04]: And when you stop doing it, that's that pleasure born of seclusion from unwholesome states that was developed beforehand.

313
00:28:04,783 --> 00:28:07,386
[SPEAKER_04]: How this... how the Pali script has described jhāna as well.

314
00:28:13,425 --> 00:28:19,172
[SPEAKER_04]: No more temptations how we drew them onto the field that's not surrounded by other people's crops and so on.

315
00:28:20,513 --> 00:28:21,194
[SPEAKER_04]: I trained them.

316
00:28:21,374 --> 00:28:22,596
[SPEAKER_04]: They never wander away.

317
00:28:23,477 --> 00:28:24,138
[SPEAKER_04]: They go a bit.

318
00:28:24,218 --> 00:28:24,919
[SPEAKER_04]: They come back.

319
00:28:25,833 --> 00:28:35,600
[SPEAKER_04]: I would allow them, I would bring them, I would allow them, I would bring them, I would allow them, I would bring them, now I allow them and as they come back on their own, I don't need to go and bring them, I don't need to allow them, they won't go anywhere.

320
00:28:36,321 --> 00:28:41,585
[SPEAKER_04]: Let me just sit and rest while thoughts and pondering is here around me, surrounding me.

321
00:28:42,866 --> 00:28:44,367
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's exactly what the first jhāna is.

322
00:28:45,688 --> 00:28:46,068
[SPEAKER_04]: That's it.

323
00:28:46,088 --> 00:28:49,871
[SPEAKER_04]: The work, the pleasure of that rest comes after you did the work.

324
00:28:50,808 --> 00:28:55,652
[SPEAKER_04]: Not how, again, the whole commentary in today's practice expects it to be.

325
00:28:55,732 --> 00:28:57,774
[SPEAKER_04]: One, I'm deeply immersed in sensuality.

326
00:28:58,094 --> 00:29:06,661
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm going to find a direct pathway, access to a meditation technique, to some pleasure of a different kind, and that's why I will forsake all of this.

327
00:29:07,862 --> 00:29:09,183
[SPEAKER_04]: That's another sensual pleasure.

328
00:29:10,624 --> 00:29:17,810
[SPEAKER_03]: So, I studied my mind internally, settled it, brought it to singleness and composure, so that my mind would not be disturbed.

329
00:29:17,830 --> 00:29:18,671
[SPEAKER_03]: And that's what, eh...

330
00:29:20,805 --> 00:29:24,688
[SPEAKER_03]: We would say it's the first jhāna, basically.

331
00:29:25,689 --> 00:29:31,734
[SPEAKER_03]: And the simile is of the cow herd resting while watching the cows, as you've said.

332
00:29:35,918 --> 00:29:38,120
[SPEAKER_03]: He just has to be mindful that the cows are there.

333
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:43,765
[SPEAKER_03]: So, too, there was need for me only to be mindful that those states were there.

334
00:29:45,463 --> 00:29:50,585
[SPEAKER_03]: Persistence, diligence was manifested in me, and recollection was not forgotten.

335
00:29:51,305 --> 00:29:55,187
[SPEAKER_03]: My body was calm and unagitated, my mind settled and elevated.

336
00:29:56,427 --> 00:30:01,749
[SPEAKER_03]: And then the āgama goes into description of second jhāna, third jhāna, fourth jhāna.

337
00:30:01,929 --> 00:30:02,349
[SPEAKER_04]: After that?

338
00:30:02,469 --> 00:30:02,690
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah.

339
00:30:03,730 --> 00:30:10,092
[SPEAKER_03]: Whereas the Pādhi goes into just the standard first, second, third, fourth.

340
00:30:10,232 --> 00:30:11,553
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that sense, you mean to say that

341
00:30:13,641 --> 00:30:19,771
[SPEAKER_02]: Quite contrary to belief that jhāna is, in that sense, even more intentional.

342
00:30:22,152 --> 00:30:29,896
[SPEAKER_04]: Instead of waiting for it... Well, the development of withdrawal from unwholesome states, yeah, it's the development.

343
00:30:29,936 --> 00:30:35,339
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a magical kind of thing that would happen on account of this observance or that observance.

344
00:30:35,860 --> 00:30:38,401
[SPEAKER_04]: That's why, you know, the monk trains himself thus.

345
00:30:38,641 --> 00:30:39,442
[SPEAKER_04]: He abandons this.

346
00:30:39,822 --> 00:30:46,685
[SPEAKER_04]: He arouses diligence to not tolerate the arisen thoughts of sensuality, but actually work on the context of danger in regard to them.

347
00:30:46,946 --> 00:30:49,147
[SPEAKER_04]: How do you tolerate thoughts of sensuality, by the way?

348
00:30:49,607 --> 00:30:56,731
[SPEAKER_04]: You can tolerate thoughts of sensuality by actively accepting them, delighting them, thinking them, fantasizing them further, acting out of them.

